unsigned char *)dataB->data, &bl, (unsigned char *)dataB->data, bl)`\n   - `dataB->data` = old `headerB->data` = "Proc-Type: 4,ENCRYPTED\\nDEK-Info: AES-128-CBC,0123456789ABCDEF\\n"\n   - `bl` = hl = length of this string\n   - `EVP_DecodeUpdate` processes this as base64 → will fail because \':\' and \',\' are not valid base64\n   - Returns -1 → goto err\n\nSo this path goes to err. No crash.\n\nBut wait, what if the header text happens to be valid base64? Let me think...\n\nActually, the header text contains characters like \':\', \',\', and \'-\' which are NOT valid base64 characters. So `EVP_DecodeUpdate` will always fail on header text.\n\nUnless... the header text is crafted to be valid base64. But that would require specific header content.\n\nHmm, let me think about another scenario. What if the PEM has a header but no DEK-Info? Like:\n\n```\n-----BEGIN CERTIFICATE-----\nProc-Type: 4,ENCRYPTED\n\nYWJjZGVmZ2hpamtsbW5vcA==\n-----END CERTIFICATE-----\n```\n\n1. Header loop:\n   - Line 2: "Proc-Type: 4,ENCRYPTED\\n" → copied to headerB\n   - Line 3: "\\n" → empty line, break\n2. Data loop reads the base64 and end marker\n3. Normal flow\n\nThis works fine.\n\nLet me think about what happens when the header contains binary data or special characters...\n\nActually, I think I need to look at this from the perspective of the `BIO_gets` function. What if the input contains null bytes? `BIO_gets` might handle them differently.\n\nWait, I just realized something. Let me look at the `BIO_new_mem_buf` function. It creates a memory BIO from a buffer. The buffer might contain null bytes. `BIO_gets` reads until it finds a newline or reaches the end of the buffer.\n\nBut the key question is: what happens when `BIO_gets` encounters a null byte in the middle of the buffer?\n\nActually, let me look at the `BIO_gets` implementation for memory BIOs.\n\nActually, I think I should just try to build the fuzzer and test various inputs. Let me try a different approach - build with ASAN